<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Fleet.Conversations</RootNamespace>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    MySqlConnector and explicit SQL, NOT EF Core.

    Two independent reasons, either sufficient. The #276 transactions depend on
    `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`, `FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED` and `ON DUPLICATE KEY UPDATE … GREATEST(…)`,
    which are expressed directly here rather than through a provider's translation of them. And the
    target framework settles it anyway: this assembly targets net10.0, while the EF MySQL provider
    this repository pins is an EF Core 9 release with no EF Core 10 counterpart.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The journal's object store (#388). MinIO is the deployment's bucket, and this is the only
      S3-compatible client in the repository.

      4.0.103.4, and two of its defaults are overridden rather than inherited, in
      `S3ObjectStore`: `ForcePathStyle` (MinIO addresses the bucket in the path, so virtual-host
      style would request a hostname that does not resolve) and both checksum properties (the SDK's
      default appends a CRC-32 trailer that MinIO 2024+ rejects on PutObject). The journal's
      integrity check is the SHA-256 the server computes over the bytes it received, so dropping
      the SDK's trailer costs nothing.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="AWSSDK.S3" Version="4.0.103.4" />

    <PackageReference Include="MySqlConnector" Version="2.4.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="RabbitMQ.Client" Version="7.1.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting.Abstractions" Version="10.0.5" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.5" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" Version="10.0.5" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Fleet.Conversations.Contracts\Fleet.Conversations.Contracts.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Fleet.Protocol\Fleet.Protocol.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Fleet.Shared\Fleet.Shared.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The migration scripts ship INSIDE the assembly. A runner that read them off disk would apply
    whatever happened to be beside the binary, and the checksum guard that makes an edited migration
    a refusal rather than a silent divergence would be checking the wrong bytes.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The maintenance loop's tick is internal and takes `now` as a parameter, so its sequencing —
      measure, arm, stamp, scan — is testable without waiting out a two-minute grace window. The
      order of those steps is the mechanism, and it was wrong once already.
    -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Fleet.Conversations.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <EmbeddedResource Include="Migrations\*.sql" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
